  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 222 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\n(Monday Night - Tuesday)\n\nSpread has decreased within model guidance with respect to the timing \nof the cold front late Monday night - Tuesday. The GFS has trended \nfaster, and now is more in line with the ECMWF. The front is \nexpected to move south of the area now closer to midday Tuesday. The \nair mass behind this front is not terribly cool though by any \nstretch, and similar to many fronts in mid/late summer, the \ndewpoints don't drop precipitously behind the boundary. That means \nanother day of heat and humidity, though it should be nothing like \ntoday (or even Monday for that matter). Peak heat index values \nof 100-105F are more likely along/south of I-70, but exactly how\nwidespread these values will be will depend on the exact timing\nof the front (as well as clouds, etc). Given there is still \nthis uncertainty, held off on any extensions to heat products as\nboth the area and the product type itself - warning or advisory\nare not clear.\n\n\n\n(Tuesday Night - Next Sunday)\n\nConfidence remains high in a definitive end to the oppressive heat \nand humidity areawide by Wednesday with the cold front expected to \nbe well to our south. The incoming air mass though is one \ncharacterized by 850-hPa temperatures and surface dewpoints closer \nto normal. Not surprisingly, high and low temperatures also should \nbe close to seasonal normals. While not exactly \"cool\", it will feel \nabout 20+ degrees cooler than peak heat index values the past 48 \nhours. Relief indeed. \n\nHigh temperatures are also expected to further cool by Friday due to \nan increase in shower and thunderstorm chances. For ~5 days out, \nthere is a pretty good signal for a possible MCS to impact portions \nof the area late Thursday night into Friday. This definitely looks \nlike the best chance (40-60%) for a widespread rain over the next 7 \ndays.",
